arospkg 0.6.0
=============

Find, install, start, update and remove software for AROS, with apkg in
the Shell or PkgManager on the desktop.

INSTALL
-------

1. Unpack the archive for your CPU and ABI.
2. Close PkgManager and let any apkg operation finish.
3. In the Shell, enter the unpacked drawer and run:

     Copy apkg C:
     apkg --version
     apkg update

Already on 0.4.x to 0.6.0? Run "apkg self-update" twice instead: the first
run replaces apkg, the second updates PkgManager and the rest of its
drawer. If the apkg you run is the copy in C:, name the drawer the second
time:

     apkg self-update --drawer "Work:arospkg-0.6.0.x86_64-aros-v11"

From this version on, one "apkg self-update", or About > Update arospkg in
PkgManager, updates both. Keep your existing SYS:Packages. Versions 0.3.x
need the manual copy above.

PkgManager is included in the x86_64 ABIv11, mainline and Pi archives.
Keep the unpacked drawer and start PkgManager from its icon, or:

     Run PkgManager

Click Update index, select a package and click Install, then Run.

BUILDS
------

0.5.0:
  x86_64-aros-v11    apkg and PkgManager: stable (AROS One)
  i386-aros-v0       apkg: checked under QEMU, see the release notes
  x86_64-aros-v1     apkg and PkgManager: experimental (mainline AROS)
  aarch64-aros-raspi apkg and PkgManager: experimental, compiled and
                     linked, NOT run on a Raspberry Pi. Please test and
                     report what works.

Downloads: https://github.com/tomaszstaniak/arospkg/releases

x86_64 ABIv11 has the most test coverage, on AROS One 1.3 under QEMU.
What was run on each other build is in the release notes. Nothing was run
on real hardware. The Folio checksum failure reported on a Pi remains
unexplained; --keep-rejected keeps such a download for a report.

For online operations you need working networking and the certificate
bundle ENV:SYS/Certificates/ca-bundle.crt. PkgManager needs Zune.

START HERE
----------

  apkg search game        find packages
  apkg show soliton       view details and requirements
  apkg install soliton    download, verify and install
  apkg run folio          start an installed application
  apkg open soliton       open the installed drawer in Wanderer
  apkg list               list installed packages
  apkg verify folio       check the installed files
  apkg remove soliton     remove it, keeping changed and additional files

Packages go into SYS:Packages by default. To use another root, put it
before the command:

  apkg --root "Work:Packages" install soliton
  apkg --root "Work:Packages" run soliton

Use the same root for later operations on those packages.

NEW IN 0.6.0
------------

- Packages can depend on, recommend, conflict with and replace other
  packages. apkg install shows the plan first: dependencies are installed
  before the package, a replaced package is removed after, and a package
  others need is not removed. The catalogue for 0.6 is index-v3.json.
- Larger programs: one file in a package may be up to 128 MiB (64 MiB
  before). Files are unpacked through small fixed buffers and checked
  against their recorded size and CRC, so a larger package needs no more
  memory to install.
- Test and preview releases are put in order: apkg upgrade moves from
  0.9.0-test2-r2 to 0.9.0-test4 and later to 0.9.0, and test10 comes after
  test9. Versions in other forms are still refused rather than guessed.
- apkg update says what is new in the catalogue; apkg recent lists what
  was added recently. apkg show and PkgManager say whether a build was
  reviewed automatically and where it was tested.

NEW IN 0.5.0
------------

- apkg run <id> [files] and PkgManager's Run start an application as its
  package describes: a Workbench program from its icon (its stack and
  tool types, so no Stack command is needed; files are passed as if
  dropped on it), a Shell program in your Shell with its return code.
  apkg run --check says whether and why not. A package that does not say
  how it starts still has Open folder. The description is application.json
  in the program's drawer, so "apkg run Work:Apps/Folio" also works for a
  drawer unpacked by hand.
- PkgManager: Run, Verify (the result at the top of the details), the
  files kept by Remove, installed packages listed even without a
  catalogue, and versions written the same way everywhere (0.4.1-aros2).
  ARexx: RUN and VERIFY.
- DEMONSTRATION ONLY: pictures beside the details (a few real screenshots
  shipped in the Demo drawer, not from the catalogue) and a Demo store
  with one fictional paid application. "Demo -- purchasing is not
  available yet": nothing can be bought or installed from it, and no data
  is asked for or sent.
- apkg-pack checks and writes the start information for authors.
- Ctrl-C while connecting is reported as cancelled, not as a network
  error.

NEW IN 0.4.2
------------

- apkg outdated, apkg upgrade --all, the Updates (N) list, Ctrl-C during
  downloads, About > Check for a newer version, --keep-rejected.

NEW IN 0.4.1
------------

- apkg upgrade moves to a newer version of a program, for example
  Limpet 0.1.0 to 0.2.0, with the same plan, kept files, rollback and
  recovery as a revision upgrade. Versions made of numbers and dots are
  compared as numbers (1.10 is newer than 1.9). An older version, or one
  apkg cannot order, is refused with the reason. PkgManager offers the
  same upgrades.

NEW IN 0.4
----------

- Open folder in PkgManager and apkg open.
- Package notes after installation and in the details.
- Clearer operation stages, results and removal reports.
- Larger catalogues: tested with 5,000 packages on x86_64 ABIv11.
  The parser allocates as needed, within memory and size limits.
- apkg self-update, with the previous executable kept as apkg.old.

UPDATES
-------

  apkg update                refresh the catalogue
  apkg outdated              list installed packages with updates
  apkg upgrade <id>          install a newer version or packaging revision
  apkg upgrade --all         every update that can be installed, in turn
  apkg rollback <id>         restore what the last upgrade replaced
  apkg self-update --check   check apkg and PkgManager against the release
  apkg self-update           update apkg and its PkgManager

Upgrade moves to a newer upstream version (0.1.0 to 0.2.0, 1.9 to
1.10, 0.9.0-test2-r2 to 0.9.0-test4) or to a newer packaging revision of
the same version. Versions are ordered when they are numbers and dots,
optionally with one -testN, -previewN or -rcN tag and a -rN rebuild tag;
any other change of version, and any older version, is refused with the
reason. Files you
changed are kept, as in a revision upgrade. Rollback needs the previous
archive in the cache. Close the application before either operation or removal.

Self-update keeps the previous files beside the new ones as .old; for
arospkg installed as a package, "apkg rollback arospkg" goes back. In
PkgManager: About > Check for a newer version, then Update arospkg.
PkgManager closes and starts again. Details and recovery:
https://github.com/tomaszstaniak/arospkg/blob/main/docs/self-update.md

FILES AND RECOVERY
------------------

  SYS:Packages/<id>/       installed program
  SYS:Packages/db/         installation records; do not edit
  SYS:Packages/cache/      downloaded archives

Removal keeps files you changed and files apkg did not install, and lists
them. A later install will not overwrite a drawer left behind by removal.
Move it aside first, keeping any files you need.

  apkg verify <id>         check installed files without repairing them
  apkg doctor              report unresolved operations
  apkg doctor --retry      retry recovery after an interruption

MORE OPTIONS
------------

  apkg --help             common commands
  apkg --help-all         full user help
  apkg --help-testing     diagnostic and test options

--plain removes decorations. --verbose shows more transport and path
details. --json provides machine-readable output for supported commands.
--dry-run previews install, remove, upgrade or rollback without changing
installed files; --fetch permits downloading the archive for that preview.

PkgManager provides the PKGMANAGER ARexx port while it is running.
Examples are in Examples/ARexx. The command reference is at:
https://github.com/tomaszstaniak/arospkg/blob/main/docs/arexx.md

LIMITS
------

- CPU and ABI matching is not proof that every application works.
- System requirements are checked, but dependencies are not installed
  automatically. Installer scripts and shared system files are not managed.
- Downloads use HTTPS and SHA-256 checks. The catalogue is not signed,
  and a matching hash does not establish an application's safety.

CHECKSUMS AND LICENCES
---------------------

BUILD.txt records the source commit and program hashes. apkg --version
reports the running binary's hash. Archive hashes are in SHA256SUMS on
the matching release page.

